, Bryce Walton, Harpoon Gunner, Thomas Y. Crowell Company, NY, (1968), page 20,\nHe pushed through knots of whalemen grouped with their families and friends, and surrounded by piles of luggage."], "quotes": [{"text": "his ancient knot of dangerous adversarie", "reference": "c. 1593 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedy of Richard the Third: […]”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene i]:"}, {"text": "As they sat together in small, separate knots, they discussed doctrinal and metaphysical points of belief.", "reference": "1822, [Walter Scott], Peveril of the Peak. […], volume (please specify |volume=I to IV), Edinburgh: […] Archibald Constable and Co.; London: Hurst, Robinson, and Co., →OCLC:"}, {"text": "When, for instance, he saw a knot of the ruffians on the prairie, discussing, of course, the single topic which then occupied their minds, he would, perhaps, take his compass and one of his sons, and proceed to run an imaginary line right through the very spot on which that conclave had assembled, and when he came up to them, he would naturally pause and have some talk with them, learning their news, and, at last, all their plans perfectly; and having thus completed his real survey he would resume his imaginary one, and run on his line till he was out of sight.", "reference": "1859, Henry David Thoreau, A Plea for Captain John Brown:"}, {"text": "The garden was bounded by a three-foot brick wall with a fringe of wood rails upon the top, and against this wall was leaning a stalwart police constable, surrounded by a small knot of loafers, who craned their necks and strained their eyes in the vain hope of catching some glimpse of the proceedings within.", "reference": "1887, Sir Arthur Conan Doyle, A Study in Scarlet, page 39:"}, {"text": "In the interval between Sunday School and Church when the congregation stretched its legs, I saw Atticus standing in the yard with another knot of men.", "reference": "1960 July 11, Harper Lee, chapter 15, in To Kill a Mockingbird, Philadelphia, Pa.; New York, N.Y.: J[oshua] B[allinger] Lippincott Company, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "At corners, under the lights, near drugstores, small knots of white, bright, chattering people showed teeth to each other, pawed each other, whistled for taxis, were whirled away in them, vanished through the doors of drugstores or into the blackness of side streets.", "reference": "1962, James Baldwin, Another Country, New York, N. Y.: The Dial Press, published January 1963, page 4:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A bond of union; a connection; a tie.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[I come] to crave a league of amity,\nAnd lastly, to confirm that amity\nWith nuptial knot […]", "reference": "c. 1591–1592 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Third Part of Henry the Sixt, […]”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene iii]:"}, {"text": "[I]t were very fit, […] to observe carefully this previous betrothing of ourselves, ere we knit the knot that can never be loosed.", "reference": "1646, Jos[eph] Hall, The Balme of Gilead: Or, Comforts for the Distressed, both Morall and Divine. […], London: […] M[iles] Flesher, for Nat[haniel] Butter, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "Indeed I would advise every ſingle lady, if poſſible, to attend her inamorato, pretty frequently at the card table; […] if he is haſty or pettiſh with any one else in company, she may depend on the ſame fate when once the knot is tied.", "reference": "1795, Joseph Addison, “An Essay on Card-playing”, in Interesting Anecdotes, Memoirs, Allegories, Essays, and Poetical Fragments; Tending to Amuse the Fancy, and Inculcate Morality, page 67:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(aviation, nautical) A unit of speed, equal to one nautical mile per hour.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Cedric claimed his old yacht could make 12 knots."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["kn", "kt"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(aviation) A unit of indicated airspeed, calibrated airspeed, or equivalent airspeed, which varies in its relation to the unit of speed so as to compensate for the effects of different ambient atmospheric conditions on aircraft performance.", "tags": [], "examples": ["In the early stages of reentry, due to the extremely-rarefied air at these altitudes, the space shuttle flew at only one to a few knots equivalent airspeed, even when its actual speed was many thousands of knots."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(nautical) A nautical mile.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": ["nm"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(slang) The bulbus glandis.", "tags": ["slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "cmn", "name": "Chinese Mandarin"}, "word": "狗結 /狗结"}, {"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "bulbe"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/nɒt/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/nɑt/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "knots", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "knotting", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "knotted", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "knotted", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To form into a knot; to tie with a knot or knots.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["We knotted the ends of the rope to keep it from unravelling."], "quotes": [{"text": "For many weeks about my loins I wore / The rope that haled the buckets from the well, / Twisted as tight as I could knot the noose, / And spake not of it to a single soul, / And spake not of it to a single soul, / Until the ulcer, eating through my skin, / Betray'd my secret penance, so that all / My brethren marvell'd greatly.", "reference": "1833 (date written), Alfred Tennyson, “St. Simeon Stylites”, in Poems. […], volume II, London: Edward Moxon, […], published 1842, →OCLC, page 56:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To form wrinkles in the forehead, as a sign of concentration, concern, surprise, etc.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["She knotted her brow in concentration while attempting to unravel the tangled strands."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To unite closely; to knit together.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The party of the papists in England are become more knotted, both in dependence towards Spain, and amongst themselves.", "reference": "a. 1627 (date written), Francis [Bacon], “Considerations Touching a Warre with Spaine. […]”, in William Rawley, editor, Certaine Miscellany Works of the Right Honourable Francis Lo. Verulam, Viscount S. Alban. […], London: […] I. Hauiland for Humphrey Robinson, […], published 1629, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, obsolete, rare) To entangle or perplex; to puzzle.", "tags": ["obsolete", "rare", "transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To form knots.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(intransitive) To knit knots for a fringe.", "tags": ["intransitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["bind", "tie", "knit", "attach", "join", "put together", "baffle", "flummox"], "antonyms": ["loosen", "unbind", "unknot", "untie"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/nɒt/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/nɑt/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "knots", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "knot", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "One of a variety of shore birds; red-breasted sandpiper (variously Calidris canutus or Tringa canutus).", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "My foot-boy shall eat pheasants, calvered salmons, / Knots, godwits, lampreys: I myself will have / The beards of barbels, served instead of salads […]", "reference": "c. 1610, Ben Jonson, The Alchemist:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "исландски брегобегач"}, {"language": {"code": "ca", "name": "Catalan"}, "word": "territ gros"}, {"language": {"code": "cs", "name": "Czech"}, "word": "jespák"}, {"language": {"code": "da", "name": "Danish"}, "word": "islandsk ryle"}, {"language": {"code": "nl", "name": "Dutch"}, "word": "knot"}, {"language": {"code": "fo", "name": "Faroese"}, "word": "íslandsgrælingur"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "sirri"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "isosirri"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "Knutt"}, {"language": {"code": "de", "name": "German"}, "word": "Knuttstrandläufer"}, {"language": {"code": "ga", "name": "Irish"}, "word": "cnota"}, {"language": {"code": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "piovanello maggiore"}, {"language": {"code": "mi", "name": "Māori"}, "word": "huahou"}, {"language": {"code": "nrf", "name": "Norman"}